":{"url":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/IncidenceMatrix","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The unoriented incidence matrix of a hypergraph: an n × m matrix of bits with cell (v, p) set"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"when vertex "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" is a member of column "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"p"},{"type":"text","text":". The vertices are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<vertexCount"},{"type":"text","text":" and the columns,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the hyperedges, are at positions "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<hyperedgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":". It conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":", so the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" algorithms run on it unchanged; it is not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"type":"text","text":" even when built from one,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"because a cell edit can give a column any number of bits."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"IncidenceMatrix"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A column is any set of vertices: empty columns and repeated columns are kept, and a graph’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop is the one bit at its vertex, the hyperedge {v}. (NetworkX’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidence_matrix"},{"type":"text","text":" gives a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"loop a zero column; Godsil–Royle’s entry 2 is not a bit. A zero column would forget the loop.)"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix"},{"type":"text","text":" is the directed form; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UndirectedIncidenceList"},{"type":"text","text":" is the sparse"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"representation of the same hypergraphs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Storage.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" Both orientations are stored, as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AdjacencyMatrix"},{"type":"text","text":" stores its rows: n rows of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"⌈m\/64⌉ words and m columns of ⌈n\/64⌉ words, with the row and column popcounts cached. So"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"members(ofHyperedgeAt:)"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentHyperedges(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" are each one "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"BitSet"},{"type":"text","text":" copy of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"⌈n\/64⌉ or ⌈m\/64⌉ words, a cell edit writes two bits in O(1), and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"transposed()"},{"type":"text","text":", the dual,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"swaps the two in O(1). The cost is 2·n·m\/8 bytes: "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"the matrix is for dense or small","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"incidence","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (spectral work at n·m up to about 10⁹, hypergraphs with large hyperedges). Sparse"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"incidence belongs in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UndirectedIncidenceList"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Equality compares cell for cell, with equal shape: column order is observable, because a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"column’s position is what a spectral caller indexes by. (","type":"text"},{"code":"UndirectedIncidenceList","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s equality","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ignores positions; convert to compare that way.)","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/incidencematrix"]}],"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Structure","remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/IncidenceMatrix.swift#L28","fileName":"IncidenceMatrix.swift"},"role":"symbol","externalID":"s:21IncidenceMatrixModule0aB0V","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"IncidenceMatrix"}],"title":"IncidenceMatrix","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"IncidenceMatrix"}],"symbolKind":"struct","modules":[{"name":"IncidenceMatrixModule"}]},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule"]]},"references":{"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/Se":{"type":"unresolvable","title":"Swift.Decodable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/Se"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/IncidenceMatrix/defaultMaximumDecodedWordCount":{"url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/incidencematrix\/defaultmaximumdecodedwordcount","fragments":[{"text":"static","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"defaultMaximumDecodedWordCount","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si"}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/IncidenceMatrix\/defaultMaximumDecodedWordCount","title":"defaultMaximumDecodedWordCount","abstract":[{"text":"The most words (8 bytes each) a decoded matrix may take by default: 2²⁴, 128 MiB. An","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"n × m matrix stores n·⌈m\/64⌉ + m·⌈n\/64⌉ words of bits and n + m counts, and an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix"},{"text":" twice that. A short payload can name a large shape (43 bytes","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"name 2²⁸ vertices), so decoding refuses one whose storage would pass the limit unless the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"decoder’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"userInfo[maximumDecodedWordCountKey]"},{"text":" allows it.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Hypergraph":{"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Hypergraph"}],"role":"symbol","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","title":"Hypergraph","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Hypergraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"An undirected hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"set of vertices of any size, the empty set included (Berge, "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"Hypergraphs","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", 1989). Not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and no shared parent with one: a hyperedge is not an edge. The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" module’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidenceGraph"},{"type":"text","text":" reads a hypergraph as its bipartite incidence graph (the Levi graph), which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"how the graph algorithms reach it."}],"url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/hypergraph","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Hypergraph"}]},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/s8Se